#{[#edb/ref 17592186050765 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060675} :activity/id #uuid "5052a9d6-d9c5-5df7-993c-818d53b162fb" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060938 :tutorial/content "We can use matrices to solve systems of linear equations. To do this, we need to set up the **augmented matrix** for the system.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the system of equations below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\color{red}2}x+{\\color{red}5}y={\\color{blue}-1} \\\\ {\\color{red}-3}x+{\\color{red}7}y={\\color{blue}1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the **coefficient matrix**, which consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{matrix}{\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}5} \\\\ {\\color{red}-3} & {\\color{red}7}\\end{matrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we create the **augmented matrix**. This is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc|c}{\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}5} & {\\color{blue}-1} \\\\ {\\color{red}-3} & {\\color{red}7} & {\\color{blue}1}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nIn the future, we will learn how to use coefficient and augmented matrices to solve systems of equations. But for now, let's practice setting them up." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060678}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059767 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059576 :question/id #uuid "b2196eb1-6691-549b-950a-a9bab6863a70" :question/math-academy-id "e-4683" :question/problem "What is the coefficient matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n![](images/14/14671e805c18814b31d0c65d0e53c60aae1cf52c60ad8dd146d8a3f4bc823915.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that every variable has a coefficient:\n\n![](images/e0/e0318c549ab4aa25bf2decb41e45e296406d699706c03aff7ea44724d20edece.png)\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left\\lbrack\n\t\\matrix {\n\t\t1 & -2 \\\n\t\t0 & 2 \n\t}\n\t\\right\\rbrack\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient Matrix of a System of Linear Equations"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060677}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060677 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059768 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059577 :question/id #uuid "b5967dd6-bdf9-5cd0-9639-ec2816d04256" :question/math-academy-id "e-4684" :question/problem "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n![](images/a5/a5abba041a61cad76d43d5bb13d0fd4e873eb2daf2282e8d9828b8ab79fe2ba0.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $x,y,z$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n![](images/2f/2f9ae37920ec528a700715658900bbbf3ec4c855767bf06f79d044f488a8bbf2.png)\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left\\lbrack\n\t\\matrix {\n\t\t1 & 1 & -3 \\\n\t\t-7 & -1 & 1 \\\n                2 & 1 & -2 \n\t}\n\t\\right\\rbrack\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix  is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n![](images/af/af1499610c0c312a1f5c50d99967a004ae6b3042c3a88ca20a86f922b28aaaff.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Augmented Matrix of a System of Linear Equations"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060679}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060939 :tutorial/content "We will use mostly systems with the following variables:\n\n- $x,y,z,$ or\n\n- $x_1,x_2,x_3,$ or\n\n- $a,b,c.$\n\nThese sets of variables will always have a *natural* order:\n\n- alphabetical order in the case of $x,y,z$ or $a,b,c,$ and\n\n- numerical order in the case of $x_1, x_2, x_3.$" :tutorial/title "The Order of the Variables in a Linear System"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060676}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059769 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059578 :question/id #uuid "05942b5f-c165-59c6-8b67-24bc8dd40cca" :question/math-academy-id "e-4685" :question/problem "What would be the system of equations for the following augmented matrix?\n\n![](images/4d/4d90e81043121495ae8bb7742400b2c9e705cb7d706f553a6ab2aec44b35d017.png)" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix consists of two parts: the coefficient matrix and the column of constants.\n\nThe coefficient matrix represents the coefficients in the linear system, while the column of constants represents the constants on the right-hand sides of the equations.\n\nUsing the variables $x$ and $y,$ then, the corresponding system of equations is\n![](images/3e/3e7f783023405be3ea79a740998fefac3a6ea909e9903f5ea300a9234ae63003.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the System of Equations Associated With an Augmented Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060680}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060680 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059770 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059579 :question/id #uuid "161a4327-2356-5779-92a6-8495cfffefb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-4686" :question/problem "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n![](images/d8/d89417b7eb2d000bbd81fcf4a89ca7eaa5529fc56d9b41bf6e8bb237d2eb9e16.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $a,b,c,d$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n![](images/93/93874fa93cfd9ef3df1492bbc35056c2381157740d8ca26b475474faf54ee4d1.png)\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n![](images/83/8356d9148bc06d76a5aee41e4a07651c43d94b2496d9542eb8b6e01ee52ea161.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Augmented Matrix of a Linear System With Variables Other Than x, y, z"}}] :activity/title "Systems of Equations as Augmented Matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059882}] [#edb/ref 17592186049120 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060681} :activity/id #uuid "84d7f054-fed4-524c-b241-f09c7fd42e5e" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060681 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060940 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given a system of linear equations and its respective augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can apply some operations to the augmented matrix that do not change the set of solutions for the corresponding system. These operations are called **elementary row operations**. \n\nAfter applying an **elementary row operation** to the augmented matrix, the new corresponding system will have the same solutions.\n\nThere are three types of elementary row operations:\n\n- switching any two rows: \n\n$$\nR_i \\leftrightarrow R_j\n$$\n\n- multiplying (each element of) a row by a non-zero number: \n\n$$\nR_i := k R_i,\\qquad k \\neq 0\n$$\n\n- adding a multiple of one row to another row: \n\n$$\nR_j := R_j+k R_i,\\qquad j \\neq i\n$$\n\nIn the notations above, the \"$:=$\" symbol is called the **assignment operator.** We use this to assign new values to every element of a given row.\n\nFor example, the row operation $R_1 := 2 R_1$ means, \"create a new row $1$ by taking the old row $1$ and multiplying it by $2.$\" When we do this, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}]\\,{R}_{1}:=2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 3 & 2\\cdot (-1) & 2\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA few final points on notation.\n\n- We use the similarity symbol ($\\sim$) to indicate when one matrix is transformed into another matrix using elementary row operations.\n\n- The assignment operator $:=$ is sometimes called the \"walrus operator\" because it looks like a walrus on its side!" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060682}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060682 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059771 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059580 :question/id #uuid "836f2f1b-5dd4-5dba-81cc-eb9ffe7db8d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4693" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n![](images/62/627d12115d61ddfdedf9db08330269de2137a23abceada977d039e90e5b88675.png)\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_2 := R_2 +(- 4) \\cdot R_1$ on $A?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation $R_2:=R_2 + (-4) R_1$ means that we need to add $-4$ times the first row to the second row. \n\nCarrying out this row operation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4+(-4)(1) & 2+(-4)(2) & -1+(-4)(3) \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -13 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Applying Row Operations To a Coefficients Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060683}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059772 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059581 :question/id #uuid "2ff0a6f5-f3b2-5b39-8b69-1a0c969548cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-4694" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $A,$ given by\n![](images/82/82729e1d34174d58f01547b58aa63db28ec68a4e1964ef5cd127a2167ed4a722.png) \n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ on $A?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ means that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{array}]\\,{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Applying Row Operations To an Augmented Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060684}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059773 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059582 :question/id #uuid "a24c9621-793b-5c60-9c47-2b452f0faa2b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4695" :question/problem "Which elementary row operation could transform the matrix $A$ into the matrix $B$, where \n![](images/56/56fd7e343edf6c26f36718a30f899af6c8eaf0cad5f6b07c90ffa93c19df6346.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has been changed is the $2$nd row. \n\nNow, applying the operation $R_2:=R_2+1 \\cdot R_1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4+1\\cdot (-4) & 1+1\\cdot (-2) & 2+1\\cdot 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation could be done by the operation \n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+1\\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+R_1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Appropriate Row Operation To Transform One Matrix Into Another"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060685}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060685 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059774 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059583 :question/id #uuid "f8417bbc-b748-519b-a997-fbb4aed755eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4696" :question/problem "Given that\n![](images/59/59ee8e91d058e347238320a2bd5496630f33534564f67f901d2ddf89000f0108.png) which elementary row operation could make $a_{22}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "In the given matrix, we have $a_{22}=5$ and we want to perform an elementary row operation that makes $a_{22}=0.$\n\n- We *cannot* make $a_{22}=0$ by switching two rows, because there are no rows with $0$ as the second entry.\n\n- We *cannot* multiply the second row by $0,$ because we are only allowed to multiply rows by *nonzero* constants.\n\n- However, we *can* add a multiple of another row to the $2$nd row to make $a_{22}=0.$\n\nLooking at the first row, we see that $a_{12}=1.$ If we add $-5$ times this entry to $a_{22},$ we will get $a_{22}=0$ as desired. So, the elementary row operation that we need to use is\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1.\n$$\n\nApplying this operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1+(-5)\\cdot 3 & 5+(-5)\\cdot 1 & 6+(-5)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -16 & 0 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the desired elemetary row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1,\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 -5 R_1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Appropriate Row Operation To Transform a Given Entry to a Desired Value"}}] :activity/title "Elementary Row Operations" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059883}] [#edb/ref 17592186050722 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088596} :activity/id #uuid "d11823b5-bd6a-59d3-b59c-fa3609b4e9e5" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088925 :tutorial/content "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta <2\\pi.\n$$\n\nIf we make a table of values and plot them, we can see why this is a circle. Let's label each point $A-H.$\n\n|  | $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,C\\,$ | $\\,D\\,$ | $\\,E\\,$ | $\\,F\\,$ | $\\,G\\,$ | $\\,H\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,\\theta\\,$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$ | $\\pi$ | $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}$ |\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\n| $\\,y\\,$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\n\n![](images/d5/d50cf22b3e7429924ce0039f4daa2191234e628173e6f5be789a872ffa520346.png)\n\nThe $\\theta$ domain $0\\leq \\theta<2\\pi$ ensures that we go around the entire circle. If we were to cut the $\\theta$ domain in half (i.e., $0\\leq\\theta\\leq \\pi$), we would get a semicircle.\n\nFor a general circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin $O,$ the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta <2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThis parametrization starts at the point $(r,0)$ (corresponding the $\\theta = 0$) and traverses the circle in the counterclockwise direction. This is the most common parameterization of a circle and is the one that we'll mostly use.\n\nAnother set of parametric equations for a circle of radius $r$ centered at $O$ is\n\n$$\nx=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad y=r\\cos\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta <2\\pi.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088597}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088597 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087742 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087554 :question/id #uuid "b798cfbb-89ac-5d31-adb5-5c43bd483226" :question/math-academy-id "e-5374" :question/problem "Consider the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown below. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0).$ \n\n![](images/15/156d160267551e9dbf4db42b14b1e0dbb46ab658a836a760cf31a42eb89662d3.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r = 2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088676}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088598 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087743 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087555 :question/id #uuid "140fc720-22c0-5832-afae-c255144291d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5375" :question/problem "Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that generates a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx= r\\cos\\theta+a,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta+b,\\quad  \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta+4,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta+6,\\quad\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088662}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088662 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087784 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087612 :question/id #uuid "4ffaeeac-95a0-598a-9880-166d03f810ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-6779" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f44fad12ad1f7d465423f92258caae38e0c536153ba2c3c3a6de573061c69993.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 6\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r = 6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/4b/4bd384e2b1a9d0568617ddbfd5ac7dbe9630882b45c40d7912f4809a6e0be551.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right],$ we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right].\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088677}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088664 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087786 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087616 :question/id #uuid "8bff424d-8f2e-528b-acf0-12c33958a132" :question/math-academy-id "e-6817" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta + 2,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta - 4, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+4}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^2 + (y+4)^2 = 9.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088960 :tutorial/content "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx= r\\cos\\theta+a,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta+b,\\quad  \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nFor example, the parametric equations of a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(1,-2)$ are\n\n$$\nx= 2\\cos\\theta+1,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta-2,\\quad  \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nThese parametric equations traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,-2),$ the rightmost point on the curve.\n\n![](images/69/692b62bc09342cae8b8be19f135ecb9e5b98460ad65ee13bfdddf39a7e1a5b6b.png)" :tutorial/title "The Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088598}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088677 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088961 :tutorial/content "To convert the parametric equations of a circle to a Cartesian equation, we can eliminate the parameter $\\theta$ using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the circle defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=5\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{5} & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & ={sin}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}\\theta +{sin}^{2}\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $x^2+y^2 = 25.$" :tutorial/title "Converting the Parametric Equations of a Circle to Cartesian Form"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088664}}] :activity/title "Parametric Equations of Circles" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087894}] [#edb/ref 17592186048980 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129938} :activity/id #uuid "5f586b25-2c9f-53c9-8c70-a938202d5eda" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129938 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130339 :tutorial/content "Consider the following matrices:\n![](images/10/1074870d255109821b5f36eb517f22531efbd8f5069f05c3de3f111b38befd02.svg)\n\nThe first non-zero entry on each row (from left to right) is called the **leading entry** of that row.\n\n- In the matrix $A,$ the leading entries are $a_{11}=2,$ $a_{22}=3,$ $a_{32}=1,$ and $a_{43}=5.$\n\n- In the matrix $B,$ the leading entries are $b_{11}=1,$ $b_{22}=3,$ $b_{33}=-2,$ and $b_{44}=2.$ The $5$th row has no leading entry.\n\nThe leading entries are highlighted below.\n\n![](images/6b/6b1d99269c847f7c6bc0558da347fb300dba5fa6caf373cd1ed5cc5ebacb1649.svg)" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130042}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129939 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129113 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128925 :question/id #uuid "674e7733-7a8e-5804-a867-82c74c583dcc" :question/math-academy-id "e-4687" :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n![](images/27/2701e789f5d3f6fc48fda87706add41ccb59c92d5bae4a76e84ac1abfdce6f07.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The first matrix is not in row echelon form since $a_{32} \\neq 0$ (condition II is violated).\n![](images/d8/d8725937a4f5f1cfcd36cbe4871a872187df071b54c15d2e29d9b1e3c5f9b731.svg)\n\n- The second matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/5e/5e3142b1ad4bb219db5d5b144e47fe8d1fa8d1f92c300c6f60fc145cf9424900.svg)\n\n- The third matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/93/9331fdbede7da49e2dbbc66aac0b3084b1c7acca1ac7e789eded304956a18500.svg)\n\n- The fourth matrix is not in row echelon form since, for example, the leading entry $a_{22}$ lies to the left of the leading entry $a_{13}$ (condition II is violated).\n![](images/76/76e7add6349e0b2fceddf094ac6e3e8cfd0bf1bb43a3fbee0c143b256b81f1c5.svg)"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Augmented Matrices in Row Echelon Form"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129941}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129940 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129114 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128926 :question/id #uuid "b6e4983b-c695-5944-9637-6d7547dae849" :question/math-academy-id "e-4688" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n![](images/56/564d16a59313514424d24b8c247d8a3516bfa32188292d2cbc7fee7faf2f5744.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n![](images/53/53977f4d27cf659f7d595afc4422b0c3009218fe836615eba5faf8a854124c6b.svg)\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/40/40fea7849f3603441671253bb7bc96e56be9bab6a8ddcbc94320238a0e3f529a.svg)\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/08/08ed315816def9b28fb3918a2dd103a5008815fc6a07507b64c5c20572b1c88f.svg)"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices in Row Echelon Form"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129939}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129941 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129115 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128927 :question/id #uuid "97237f8e-b317-5c86-86d1-8fd3cc83e66b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4689" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n![](images/aa/aad9e35c3e07e67260a7f3ce96da685be97fab68fe542d262be0ed4f0941f909.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that by replacing $a_{32}$, $a_{42}$, and $a_{43}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n![](images/f8/f89c32a7464b2eeb0f6db6a2c80feef98e8aa24d012b44695e7c7d24b91829ec.svg)"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Operations That Would Transform a Matrix To Row Echelon Form"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130041 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130380 :tutorial/content "Let's consider our matrices $A$ and $B$ once more, with the leading entries highlighted.\n\n![](images/af/af61f32297e248ab9989ba59ea6c9bda2f99efe19d25df525fef916617843a32.svg)\n\nWe say that a matrix is in **row echelon form** if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nHere, $B$ is in row echelon form since both conditions are satisfied. However, $A$ is not in row echelon form since condition II is violated: the leading entry of the third row $(a_{32}={\\color{red}1})$ is placed just below the leading entry of the second row $(a_{22}=3).$\n\nGenerally speaking, row echelon form means that we have *stairs* where each step (represented by a leading entry $\\bbox[lightgray, 3pt]{\\large\\ast}$) has a height of $1.$\n\n![](images/67/67f83c454e59d16ae80cf67a19b19f968182251a2e5d1b405e2c422e0313d5b7.png)\n\nFinally, an *augmented* matrix is in row echelon form if the coefficient matrix (i.e., the part that is to the left of the vertical bar) is in row echelon form." :tutorial/title "Row Echelon Form"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129940}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130042 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129176 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128995 :question/id #uuid "7fd5c01f-c20e-5c47-b8d9-8cfbe634c14d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6166" :question/problem "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n![](images/85/8579b0afd584298a247c38af153d4e69945e73923e9605a168734c9250f3d18a.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows of $A$ are $-1$, $-4$ and $8$, respectively.\n\n![](images/11/11af9a5354d23586af399f9bfade0641e9bb10898068ea825755de5c645e3d9c.svg)\n\nSo, the answer is $-1+(-4)+8=3.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Leading Entries of a Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130041}}] :activity/title "Row Echelon Form" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129395}]}
